{
  "work_item": 5,
  "measurement": "Same signed native NVK build, cold and warm Mesa shader cache",
  "parent_source": "c492a9343a28a1346deb9929e283e4e9835e0cce",
  "method": {
    "adapter": "NVIDIA GM20B on Switch Lite, matched KMT LUID and NVK driver identity. Per architecture, a single ICD and loader hash served both cache states.",
    "scene": "32x32 Vulkan triangle. For every submit, read back 1024 pixels (300 red, 724 blue) and both 64-byte guards. Each process uses a freshly created pipeline and 24 serial submissions.",
    "order": "Three pairs per architecture, nine pairs per run. The architecture order rotates in three blocks. Each pair starts with an empty, isolated directory for the cold process and reuses it in a fresh warm process.",
    "power": "AC online, Handheld policy, active DFLL, responsive adapter before and after. The sampled frequencies are dynamic.",
    "scope": "vkCreateGraphicsPipelines wall time includes driver work and cache I/O. The earlier prototype includes one GetProcessTimes call within this interval. The published tool moves that call outside the interval. Submit to fence is host-observed completion. The buffer/image stages include allocation, binding and staging setup. Neither is an isolated KMD/NAK hardware timestamp.",
    "statistics": "Each per-phase pipeline median uses three processes in its own run. Submission percentiles use nearest rank on 72 individual samples for each architecture and cache state, retaining the first submission. CPU times have scheduler tick granularity.",
    "interpretation": "This experiment measures the effect of an existing cache in the accepted driver. It does not compare driver source revisions or game FPS."
  },
